# SPIRITHAUS Social Studio — environment. Copy to .env and fill in.
# No secret belongs in this file or in the repository.

# --- Shopify ------------------------------------------------------------------
# From your Partner Dashboard. `shopify app config link` writes the client id into
# shopify.app.toml; the secret only ever lives here.
SHOPIFY_API_KEY=
SHOPIFY_API_SECRET=
SHOPIFY_APP_URL=https://localhost:3000
# Pinned deliberately. Shopify deprecates versions on a schedule, and an unpinned
# client changes behaviour without a deploy.
SHOPIFY_API_VERSION=2025-07
# Scopes are not configured here. They come from mandatoryScopeList() in
# packages/shopify/src/scopes.ts, where each one is recorded with the feature that stops
# working without it. A SCOPES variable used to sit here and was read by nothing.

# --- Data ---------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://spirithaus:spirithaus@localhost:5432/spirithaus_dev?schema=public
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
QUEUE_PREFIX=spirithaus
SYNC_CONCURRENCY=2

# --- Providers ----------------------------------------------------------------
# One variable per contract. `mock` (or unset) is a supported configuration: a mocked
# provider transmits nothing, and a mocked publisher reports "not published" rather
# than a plausible success.
#
# A named adapter that this build does not implement is refused with an explanation —
# it never silently falls back to a mock.
PROVIDER_TEXT=mock
PROVIDER_RESEARCH=mock
PROVIDER_TREND=mock
PROVIDER_COPY_SCORING=mock
PROVIDER_IMAGE=mock
PROVIDER_IMAGE_EDIT=mock
PROVIDER_AVATAR=mock
PROVIDER_VOICE=mock
PROVIDER_VIDEO_GEN=mock
PROVIDER_VIDEO_RENDER=mock
PROVIDER_MODERATION=mock
PROVIDER_PUBLISHING=mock
PROVIDER_SOCIAL_ANALYTICS=mock
PROVIDER_ATTRIBUTION=mock
PROVIDER_EMAIL=mock
PROVIDER_STORAGE=mock

# Provider credentials are deliberately not listed. No adapter reads one yet, and a list of
# placeholder key names reads as a checklist for a live deployment that nothing would honour.
# Each returns here when the adapter that consumes it exists.
#
# There is no Admin access token variable, and there will not be one: offline tokens are issued
# by OAuth and stored in the Session table. Nothing reads a token from the environment.

# --- Session encryption -------------------------------------------------------
# Shopify access and refresh tokens are encrypted before they are stored (ADR 0014). Every key
# listed can decrypt; only the current one encrypts, so adding a key and promoting it are
# separate steps and rotation never has a window where the app cannot read its own sessions.
#
# Generate one — and keep it somewhere durable:  openssl rand -base64 32
# It must decode to exactly 32 bytes. A 32-character passphrase is not a 256-bit key.
#
# LOSING EVERY VALID KEY MEANS EVERY SHOP MUST REINSTALL. There is no recovery.
#
# No key is written here, ever. On a developer machine, leaving both unset stores tokens as
# given and logs a warning; any deployed environment refuses to start without them.
# SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEYS=k1:<base64 32 bytes>
# SESSION_ENCRYPTION_CURRENT_KEY_ID=k1
#
# Development only, for reading tokens written before encryption existed. Refused outright when
# NODE_ENV is not development or test. Run `pnpm db:encrypt-sessions --apply` instead.
# SESSION_ENCRYPTION_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_READS=true

# --- Object storage (ADR 0015) ------------------------------------------------
# Masters, composites and environment plates. Fly's Tigris integration injects these on
# `fly storage create`, under exactly these names — nothing here is a SPIRITHAUS invention.
#
# LOCAL STORAGE IS DEVELOPMENT AND TEST ONLY. A deployed deployment runs the web and worker
# processes on separate machines with separate filesystems, so a local directory is not shared
# storage: the web process writes an environment plate to its own disk and the worker cannot find
# it. Every composite fails, and it fails saying the *artwork* is missing. So:
#
#   - Leave all of these unset on a developer machine: media goes to MEDIA_STORE_DIR and the
#     process warns about it.
#   - Any deployed environment (NODE_ENV neither development nor test) refuses to start without
#     them. That is deliberate and must not be worked around.
#   - Setting *some* of them is fatal everywhere. A named bucket with a missing credential is a
#     typo, and falling back to a local directory would hide it until the worker read failed.
#
# BUCKET_NAME and AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3 are what select a store. The credentials are required once
# one is selected but never select one on their own, because AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are ambient on plenty of machines that have nothing to do with this app.
#
# The bucket must stay PRIVATE. Nothing here sets an ACL and no object is written public-read; a
# protected product master is not public artwork.
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3=https://fly.storage.tigris.dev
# BUCKET_NAME=
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Optional. Defaults to `auto`, which is what Tigris expects; it routes for you.
# AWS_REGION=auto

# --- Operations ---------------------------------------------------------------
LOG_LEVEL=info
NODE_ENV=development
# Queue prefix must match between the app and the worker, or the worker watches a queue
# nothing writes to.
# QUEUE_PREFIX=spirithaus
# COMPOSITE_CONCURRENCY=2
# RENDER_CONCURRENCY=1
# Where composites and masters are written when no object storage is configured. Development and
# test only — see the object storage section below.
# MEDIA_STORE_DIR=/tmp/spirithaus-media
# Set to 1 whenever https_proxy is set: Node reads it once at startup, and without it fetch
# bypasses the proxy and an egress denial arrives looking like a rejected Shopify token.
# NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1
# PRISMA_LOG=query
# Development seed only; never set in production.
SEED_SHOP_DOMAIN=spirithaus-dev.myshopify.com
